A family with high penetration + low share = nearly everyone gets one, but only one (e.g. main checking account — 95% of clients have one, but it's one event each).
A family with moderate penetration + high share = fewer clients touch it, but those who do, buy a lot (e.g. cards — half of clients, but multiple cards each).
A family with low penetration + low share = rare and shallow product, real cross-sell opportunity (e.g. wealth management early in lifecycle).
A family with high penetration + high share = the workhorse of that lifecycle stage.



import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

# Pivot so each family is a column, months are the index
pen_pivot = window_mix_family.pivot(
    index="window_months", columns="Famille Produit", values="penetration_pct"
)
share_pivot = window_mix_family.pivot(
    index="window_months", columns="Famille Produit", values="share_of_window_pct"
)

# Sort families by their final value so the legend reads top-to-bottom
pen_order = pen_pivot.iloc[-1].sort_values(ascending=False).index
share_order = share_pivot.iloc[-1].sort_values(ascending=False).index

fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(16, 6))

# Penetration curve
pen_pivot[pen_order].plot(ax=axes[0], marker="o", linewidth=2)
axes[0].set_title("Penetration by family across lifecycle windows")
axes[0].set_xlabel("Months since joining")
axes[0].set_ylabel("Penetration (% of eligible clients)")
axes[0].set_xticks(pen_pivot.index)
axes[0].grid(alpha=0.3)
axes[0].legend(title="Famille Produit", bbox_to_anchor=(1.02, 1), loc="upper left")

# Share of window curve
share_pivot[share_order].plot(ax=axes[1], marker="o", linewidth=2)
axes[1].set_title("Share of window by family across lifecycle")
axes[1].set_xlabel("Months since joining")
axes[1].set_ylabel("Share of subscriptions in window (%)")
axes[1].set_xticks(share_pivot.index)
axes[1].grid(alpha=0.3)
axes[1].legend(title="Famille Produit", bbox_to_anchor=(1.02, 1), loc="upper left")

plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()










